    <h2>1. The Hydraulic Recirculation Catastrophe</h2>
    <p>
      In single-pump systems, one check valve is placed on the vertical discharge pipe to prevent the column of water standing in the pipe (often 10 to 12 feet high) from draining back down into the pit when the motor turns off.
    </p>
    <p>
      When homeowners install a secondary battery backup pump, they often make the mistake of running both pumps into a single tee fitting with only ONE check valve located downstream above the junction.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>What happens during a flood:</strong> When grid power drops and the 12V backup pump kicks on, water exits the backup pump and reaches the tee. Because water follows the path of least resistance, it does NOT push up the 10-foot vertical pipe against gravity! Instead, it flows straight down the idle primary pump's pipe, pushes through the idle impeller blades, and squirts back out into the sump pit! The backup pump runs nonstop, draining its 100Ah battery in 4 hours while the water level rises and floods the basement!
    </p>

    <h2>2. Dual Check Valve Architecture</h2>
    <p>
      To prevent reverse hydraulic recirculation, you must install <strong>two dedicated check valves</strong>:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Check Valve 1:</strong> Mounted on the vertical 1-1/2" discharge pipe of the Primary 120V AC pump, inside the basin or immediately above the lid.</li>
      <li><strong>Check Valve 2:</strong> Mounted on the vertical 1-1/2" discharge pipe of the Secondary 12V DC pump, before joining the primary pipe.</li>
      <li><strong>The Junction Rule:</strong> Join the two pipes together using a <strong>wye and 45-degree elbow</strong> (directional flow), NEVER a 90-degree tee. A 90-degree tee generates severe hydraulic turbulence, creating 4 to 6 feet of additional friction head loss that reduces the backup pump's flow rate by up to 25%.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. The Physics of the 3/16" Air Weep Relief Hole</h2>
    <p>
      Why does a brand-new, powerful 1/2 HP pump suddenly fail to pump water even though the motor is running? The culprit is almost always an <strong>airlock</strong>:
    </p>
    <div class="bg-amber-50 border-l-4 border-amber-600 p-4 text-xs leading-relaxed text-amber-900 my-4">
      When the basin empties and the pump shuts down, water drains back down from the check valve flapper, sealing the check valve tightly shut. Air enters the bottom of the pump casing. When groundwater rises again, water surrounds the exterior of the pump, trapping an internal pocket of air inside the impeller volute. A centrifugal pump impeller CANNOT compress air—it can only pump dense liquid! The spinning impeller simply cavitates in the dry air bubble, generating zero suction.
    </div>
    <p>
      <strong>The Solution:</strong> Drill a <strong>3/16-inch weep relief hole</strong> through the PVC discharge pipe, exactly 2 to 3 inches above the pump outlet and BELOW the check valve. As water rises in the pit, trapped air vents harmlessly through this hole, allowing water to flood the impeller volute so the pump primes instantaneously. Angle the hole downward at 45 degrees so the small stream of water during pumping shoots toward the bottom of the pit.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Standard Swing Flapper vs. Spring-Loaded Silent Check Valves</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-xs border border-gray-200 rounded-lg">
        <thead class="bg-gray-100 text-gray-700 uppercase font-semibold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Feature</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Standard Gravity Swing Flapper</th>
            <th class="p-3 border-b">Spring-Loaded "Silent" Check Valve</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200 bg-white">
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Closing Mechanism</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Gravity &amp; reverse water backflow</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Internal stainless steel spring</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Water Hammer Slam</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-red-600 font-bold">Loud 80+ dB bang (shakes wall pipes)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Near-silent closure (Zero water hammer)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Flapper Wear / Tear</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">High (Plastic hinge pins crack over time)</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Low (Guided elastomeric poppet seal)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="p-3 font-medium text-gray-900">Coupling Connection</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">Glue-on PVC slip fittings</td>
            <td class="p-3 text-emerald-700 font-bold">Rubber Fernco boots with stainless clamps (easy removal)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
